# 家庭服务器分流 / Home lab routing
# Updated: 2026-08-06
#
# Why this list exists:
#   The company network runs DPI that RST-injects on the SSH identification
#   banner. The TCP handshake to the NAS completes, then:
#     kex_exchange_identification: read: Connection reset by peer
#   Changing the listening port does not help — the block keys on the protocol
#   fingerprint, not the port. Wrapping the session in the proxy's TLS hides
#   that fingerprint, so SSH / RDP / DSM all come back.
#
# MUST be registered as the FIRST line of [filter_remote]:
#   The home line is China Telecom AS4134. ASN.China.list (tag=国内网站) carries
#   5077 IP-ASN rules with zero no-resolve, and IP-ASN,4134 is its first entry.
#   Without no-resolve, Quantumult X resolves these host names, matches AS4134,
#   and applies that list's force-policy=direct — sending the traffic straight
#   back out over the company link and into the DPI. Any filter registered after
#   国内网站, and anything in [filter_local], never gets a turn.
#
# Target policy: 公司受限
#   [policy]
#   static = 公司受限, 自动选择, direct, 日本节点, 狮城节点, proxy, img-url=<Server.png>
#
#   Deliberately shared with 公司音乐媒体分流.list: both lists exist for the same
#   reason — the company network blocks the destination — so they flip together.
#   One group means one switch. Splitting them again is just a matter of giving
#   each filter its own force-policy.
#
#   Defaults to the proxy, so the filter stays enabled=true; switch the group to
#   direct by hand at home, where the extra hop buys nothing.
#
#   An `ssid` group was tried first, to flip automatically on the three company
#   networks (gnaixeuy / Linklogis_Guest / Linklogis_Phone). On macOS its SSID
#   mappings never matched: whatever sat in the two default slots was applied and
#   every `SSID:policy` entry was silently ignored — confirmed by swapping the
#   defaults and watching the group follow the default rather than the mapping.
#   Not a permissions problem: Quantumult X ships the
#   com.apple.developer.networking.wifi-info entitlement and declares no
#   NSLocation* keys, so it never appears under 定位服务 and does not need to.
#   Policy-group references are accepted in the default slots, so the group name
#   was not the issue either. Untested on iOS, where `ssid` may well work.
#
# Scope discipline:
#   Only the host names that are actually home. gnaixeuy.cn as a whole is NOT
#   home — a HOST-SUFFIX on the apex would drag unrelated services on the same
#   zone (blog, status page, sub endpoint) through the proxy. Add new entries one
#   exact HOST at a time.

# ========= 家里的具体主机 / Home hosts (exact match only) =========
HOST,nas.gnaixeuy.cn,公司受限
HOST,home.gnaixeuy.cn,公司受限
HOST,home_v6.gnaixeuy.cn,公司受限

# ========= 按 IP 直连时的兜底 / Fallback for connections made by raw IP =========
# HOST rules match only when the client dials the host name. An RDP or SSH
# client pointed at a bare address bypasses them entirely, so pin the address
# here too. The home line is dynamic DDNS, so treat these as disposable:
#   dig +short nas.gnaixeuy.cn
# re-check whenever the tunnel stops working, and update the value.
#
# Last observed IPv4 (2026-08-06, CHINANET-FJ / AS4134):
# IP-CIDR,110.90.204.49/32,公司受限,no-resolve
#
# IPv6 equivalent. Pin the /64 rather than a single address — the interface half
# rotates under SLAAC privacy extensions. Note the profile currently sets
# no-ipv6 in [dns], so an IPv6-only entry point will not resolve until that is
# removed and 兼容性增强 is enabled in the app.
# IP6-CIDR,2408:xxxx:xxxx:xxxx::/64,公司受限,no-resolve
#
# no-resolve is required on both: without it Quantumult X performs a DNS lookup
# to test the rule, which leaks the query and slows every unmatched connection.
